    "case_id": "633",
    "case_summary": "A 20-year-old, Spanish-speaking Guatemalan man without significant medical history was admitted with one week of new, progressive headache associated with nausea and vomiting. He did not report an elevated temperature or respiratory symptoms. On further history taking, the patient disclosed immigrating to the United States by traveling through Mexico.\n\nPhysical examination findings, including mental status and neurologic, pulmonary, abdominal, and skin examinations, were normal.\n\n- Laboratory studies:    * Mild anemia (11.1 g/dL)    * Normal leukocyte level without eosinophilia    * Hypotonic hyponatremia (127 mmol/L)    * Normal electrolytes, glucose level, blood urea nitrogen level, creatinine level, and liver function test results    * Negative HIV serology    * Serum complement-fixation IgG antibody titers of 1:512  - Imaging Studies:    * Initial CT head: Subarachnoid hemorrhage and mild cerebral edema    * Follow-up CT (Day 14): Well-defined subarachnoid space hyperdensity and worsening overlying edema    * MRI head: Diffuse leptomeningeal thickening    * CT chest: Left lower lobe nodular consolidation and lymphadenopathy    * Cerebral angiogram: Negative for underlying cause    * Transthoracic echocardiogram: Negative for underlying cause  - CSF Analysis:    * Opening pressure: 50 cmH₂O    * Leukocyte level: 208/mL³ with lymphocytic pleocytosis without eosinophilia    * Red blood cells: 8/mL³    * Protein: 409 mg/dL    * Glucose: 10 mg/dL    * No xanthochromia    * Complement-fixation IgG antibody titers: 1:8    * Negative bacterial culture and mycobacterial PCR testing",
